 the supervised layer  so i'll start with candidate selection  now why do we need indexing so the main  one of the main reasons why we need  indexing is to reduce the computational  complexity  so if you take my previous example of  matching 3 million records to  13 million records in our ground truth  data it will generate  39 billion 39 trillion record pair  comparisons  but as i said a bulk of those name pairs  are irrelevant  or it's very easy to filter out obvious  mismatches from that  so the idea of indexing is to first  filter out obvious mismatches from the  data  and to move similar records close  together so that  further down in the line of further down  in the entity matching process  you have very few record comparisons to  make  so this is the current uh pipeline that  we have for  name matching algorithm so we have two  sets of records  two sets of names that we want to match  with each other  so first we talk nice the names in both  the data sets  using n-grams or word grams and then we  convert that into vectors  using term frequency inverse document  frequency matrix  and then we compute cosine similarity on  the term frequency inverse document  frequency matrices so it is important to  note here  that this is not a typical nlp pipeline  where we are trying to  find it semantic information from the  data so we have two sets of names  and other information related with that  entity  so all we are trying to do is to  identify the sub strings or subgrams  that is present in both set of names and  to do a  similarity computation on top of that so  at ing we have already developed a  package called sparse.top in  which computes the cosine similarity on  large data sets in a very efficient  manner  but it still has the problem that it is  of quadratic complexity or that it  basically goes through  all the records in both the data sets  and does  many record pair comparisons so we are  trying to reduce that  by ensuring that we get a high recall or  recall good enough  added on top of this candidate selection  technique  so that's where so the neighborhood  indexing comes in  so it's a very simple and efficient  technique to create  candidate pairs or relevant candidate  pairs between two sets of names  so i'll briefly explain how sort of  neighborhood indexing is implemented  so we have two sets of names the one  which is shown in  red color and another set of names shown  in green color  so now in order to implement certain  neighborhood indexing  we create something similar to an  inverted index by combining the set of  names in both the sets  and sorting it in alphabetical order and  once it's sorted in alphabetical order  we fix a window link for sort  neighborhood indexing  and the window moves over the sorted  sorted  list and find candidate pairs within the  window  so it looks at names or name pairs  within the window  and if either of the names belong to  either data sets then it will create  name pairs between them  so it will go through the window  basically moves through the entire list  and the image on the right shows the  candidate base which is generated  for the names which i've shown so the  circles  in red show the names from the first  data set and the circles in green  show the names from the second data set  and the lines between the circles  or the notes show the candidate pairs  which are created between  different sets of names in both the data  sets  so sort of neighborhood indexing works  pretty well when both data sets have  similar size or similar length  and it is also particularly useful in  identifying candidate pairs with partial  matches  which cosine similarity is not really  good at  so to give an example let's say we have  a name pair where one of the names is  company name and another name is company  name  plus subsidy name so now when we try to  compute consent similarity between the  set of names  then it will give a very low question  similarity score because it's only a  