ood  indexing may not catch that  so the idea is to enhance or apply this  different data enhancements  prior to applying sni and once you apply  sni when you combine all these different  data enhancements and then sni then it  will give  give pretty good boost in recall  so here's another comparison puts out  the neighborhood indexing  for a window length of 13 and applying  different data enhancements prior to  that  so if you look at the s and i baseline  it gave us a recall of around 44.08  percent  now there's another record which is  highlighted here that is reversing the  names and all we are doing there is  reversing the names in both the sets  now after applying sni after reversing  the names  it gave us a recall of 54.46 percent and  that's a huge boost  compared to the baseline sni because it  gave around 10  increase in recall so  one thing to note here is that depending  on the data that you're dealing with  when you do certain enhancements you get  pretty good boost in recall and since  sni itself doesn't take a lot of time to  compute  you can apply this data enhancements and  then do s and i  on top of it so to compare it with  cosine similarity  um computation so cosine similarity gave  us a recall of around fifty point nine  six percent  with a precision of five point five one  percent  and combining different data  enhancements and applying sni  on top of it gave us a recall of fifty  nine point zero four percent and a  precision of  one point zero four percent so here even  though the precision is very low for sni  we don't have to worry about it  because we know that or we have seen  that in the supervised layer  we are able to filter out the incorrect  matches  pretty easy pretty easily and  combining the multiple data enhancements  with sni  gave us a boost in recall of around  eight percent which is  pretty good so here's another  comparison of the execution time for  different candidate selection techniques  so on x-axis we have different lengths  of ground truth data and on y-axis we  have the execution time  so the yellow curve shows the execution  time for  s and i with a window length of 13 and  the gray and  red curve show execution time for cosine  similarity word based  and in gram based and as you can see  s is scaling pretty well and for a  ground rule data of 3 million records  sn8 takes only 16 minutes compared to  more than two  two hours for question similarity in  gram-based technique  so sni is a very fast candidate  selection technique  compared to similarity in gram and word  based  so some of the key takeaways here  combining multiple candidate selection  techniques actually help improve the  recall  and sni is a very simple technique to  add recall  and one of the reasons why we need to  combine multiple candidate selection  techniques is because  different candidate selection techniques  help in identifying different kinds of  name pairs  and that's what gives the boost in  recall  and on testing it on security exchange  commission data set  we can see that we got a an improvement  in recall by around eight percent  and also uh sni is particularly useful  when you have the ground flow data which  is not too large  so now i'll discuss name clustering  which is a technique  to enhance the ground rule data  so why do we need name clustering as i  said our goal is to match all the names  associated with an entity to the name in  ground truth  this but as i showed before we typically  have only one name that's present in  ground rule data  so what we're proposing here is to apply  clustering on the set of unique names  associated with an entity  and pick the cluster seed names and add  that as an alternate name in the ground  truth so that in future when we do the  matches we have  a higher recall or we are able to do  more matches  so i'll explain that in a bit more  detail so  it's same as the previous example where  we have ground truth  uh mr clean unilever and png and in the  names 