 with an entity  so here we would basically have it's  important to note that  each name would only belong to one  cluster and the basic requirement for us  here  is to have a highly efficient algorithm  and  also another thing to note here is that  the clustering algorithm that we have  implemented is quite similar to the  canopy clustering algorithm  that peter kristen has described in his  indexing techniques research paper  so currently we have a very simple and  fast algorithm to do clustering  and to separate out different sets of  names  in the external set and then add as an  alternate  ground root name so now how do we set  the threshold function  the threshold function is just a  criteria to separate out obvious  mismatches in the data  and in our case we basically look at  combination of concentrative word based  and in gram based  so here this is a plot of cosine  similarity values for positive name  pairs  and on x-axis we show cosine similar to  word-based and on y-axis we have  question similarity  three gram-based now since these are  positive name pairs  we would expect almost all the points to  be concentrated in the bottom right  corner  top right corner but as you can see we  still have records in the bottom left  corner  because why we expect them to be  concentrated on the top right is because  these are positive name pairs and almost  all the values of cosine similarities  should be on the higher range  so since we are seeing values in the  bottom left corner we know for a fact  that  these are obvious mismatches or these  are quite different  so if we take the example of mr proper  we would basically have mr proper with  mr proper  and mr proper so that particular name  pair  being on the top right corner and  mr clean and don limpio name pair being  on the  bottom left corner which clearly says  that they  can be separated out in the clustering  so we apply  a simple and very loose threshold  function based on this  basically the criteria saying that if a  particular  question similarity for a particular  name pair if the cosensum like the word  based and in gram-based values  are falling within this threshold then  you separate it out into different  candidate pairs and this is based upon  the data that we have  so depending on the data that you deal  with you can also use any threshold  function  that set the criteria for names to be  separated out into  two different clusters  so we apply clustering on security  exchange commission data set  and these are some of the examples of  clustering and as you can see in the  first example  cluster zero start with name starting  with alan test  and we have cluster one with names  starting with test edger  and as you can see um let's say  typically we only have allen test  as a ground truth name for this  particular entity  then we won't be able to match test  edgar whereas after applying clustering  we know that test edger  should be added should be added as an  alternate ground truth name  so in future we would be able to match  this and the same applies for  the other example of don limpio on the  right as well  so we would add don limpio mr proper  monsieur proper  as alternate groundhog name so that we  would be able to find more matches and  thus  boost recall so we basically applied  clustering on all the names associated  with all the entities in our external  set  and enhance the ground rule data with  respect to that  so here i'm basically comparing the  enhancement of ground truth techniques  based upon candidate selection  so as i said we typically have only one  name that's present in the ground road  and after applying cluster we pick the  cluster seed names  and add them as alternate ground truth  name  and apply candidate selection on top of  that so the plot that you see here on x  axis we have different ground root  specification and on y  axis we have the maximum recall so the  first set of bars  is the candidate selection with respect  to 